The network shows direct flight routes between airports.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (P) at (2,1.5);
\coordinate (Q) at (0,3);
\coordinate (R) at (4,3);
\coordinate (S) at (5,1.5);
\coordinate (T) at (4,0);
\coordinate (U) at (0,0);
\draw (P)--(Q);
\draw (P)--(R);
\draw (P)--(S);
\draw (P)--(T);
\draw (P)--(U);
\draw (Q)--(R);
\draw (T)--(U);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (T) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (U) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below] at (P) {$P$};
\node[above left] at (Q) {$Q$};
\node[above right] at (R) {$R$};
\node[right] at (S) {$S$};
\node[below right] at (T) {$T$};
\node[below left] at (U) {$U$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) What does the degree of a vertex represent?
(b) How many direct flight routes are available from $P$?
(c) Which airport has the most direct flight routes?
(d) How many vertices have odd degree, and how many have even degree?

The network shows roads between towns. The diagonal roads cross at an overpass with no connection; no vertex is marked there.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2.6);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.6);
\coordinate (E) at (4.4,2.6);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--(A);
\draw (A)--(C);
\draw (B)--(D);
\draw (C)--(E);
\foreach \v in {A,B,C,D,E}{\fill[black] (\v) circle (2.5pt);}
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(a) Count the vertices and edges.
(b) Find the sum of the degrees by listing each vertex's degree, then check using the number of edges.
